<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Series as functions</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>We can use the fact that the geometric series gives us
<md>
\frac1{1-x} = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \ldots + x^n + \ldots
</md>
when <m>-1&lt;x&lt;1</m> to find series representations of other functions.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's begin by rewriting the geometric series in terms of the
  variable <m>u</m>, rather than <m>x</m>;  that is, set <m>x=u</m> to write the
  series for <m>\displaystyle \frac1{1-u}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now let <m>u = -x</m> to obtain a series for <m>\displaystyle
  \frac1{1+x}</m>.  Write the first four nonzero terms and try to write
  the general term.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now that we have a series for <m>\displaystyle \frac1{1+x}</m>, let's rewrite
  this in terms of the variable <m>u</m>, as we did before.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why <m>\displaystyle \ln(1+x) = \int_0^x \frac{1}{1+u}~du</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>By integrating the series you found for <m>\displaystyle\frac1{1+u}</m>, find a
  series representation of <m>\ln(1+x)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>x=1/2</m> to obtain a series for <m>\ln(3/2)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Approximate the value of <m>\ln(3/2)</m> using the first five terms in
  your series.  How does your approximation compare to the actual
  value?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's start again with the series for <m>\displaystyle
  \frac1{1-u}</m>.  Write this series below and substitute <m>u=-x^2</m> to
  produce a series for <m>\displaystyle \frac1{1+x^2}</m>.  Write the first
  four nonzero terms and try to write the general term.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Remember that <m>\arctan x = \displaystyle\int_0^x \frac1{1+u^2}~du</m> and
  use this to create a series for <m>\arctan x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Remember that <m>\displaystyle \frac\pi4 = \arctan 1</m>
  and use this to write a series for <m>\pi/4</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What approximation to <m>\pi/4</m> do you find with the first five terms?
  How does this compare to the actual value?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Using <m>x = 1/2</m>, write a series that gives <m>\arctan(1/2)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What approximation to <m>\arctan(1/2)</m> do you find with the first five
  terms?  How does this compare to the actual value?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
